Chapter: AddrOfProbe

 What does `address-of` answer, and is that answer an identity?

 `address-of` is typed `forall a. a -> Integer` and emitted on bare metal by
 `emit-identity-builtin` -- the value itself, no load. `variant-tag` emits
 `mov-load rd, r, 0` instead, dereferencing word 0. So a variant is a pointer
 and address-of hands back that pointer -- IF a payload-free variant is boxed
 at all. If it is instead an immediate tag word, then `address-of MA` is 0 for
 the first constructor, and the compiler's own `address-of m == 0` absence
 idiom (Types/Unifier.codex, ten sites) reads a real value as absent.

 The two `WSome 5` lines are the identity question rather than the value
 question: `mkey-type` mixes address-of answers into a hash-cons key that
 `cons-probe-at` matches in FULL and then adopts on, with no structural
 re-check. That is only sound if equal structure gives equal answers and
 distinct structure gives distinct ones. Two separately built `WSome 5` say
 which half, if either, holds here.

Section: Subjects

  Mode =
   | MA
   | MB
   | MC

  Wrapped =
   | WNone
   | WSome (Integer)

Section: Main

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni ("payload-free MA addr " & show (address-of MA))
   print-line-uni ("payload-free MB addr " & show (address-of MB))
   print-line-uni ("payload-free MC addr " & show (address-of MC))
   print-line-uni ("payload-free MA tag  " & show (variant-tag MA))
   print-line-uni ("payload-free MB tag  " & show (variant-tag MB))
   print-line-uni ("payload-free MC tag  " & show (variant-tag MC))
   print-line-uni ("mixed WNone addr     " & show (address-of WNone))
   print-line-uni ("mixed WSome 5 addr   " & show (address-of (WSome 5)))
   print-line-uni ("mixed WSome 5 addr   " & show (address-of (WSome 5)))
   print-line-uni ("integer 7 addr       " & show (address-of 7))
   print-line-uni ("integer 0 addr       " & show (address-of 0))
   print-line-uni ("text addr            " & show (address-of "hello"))
  end
